plain":["Loading checkpoint shards:   0%|          | 0/2 [00:00<?, ?it/s]"],"application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json":{"version_major":2,"version_minor":0,"model_id":"98969436672a4d5c9d2fb6fad4373aed"}},"metadata":{}},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["generation_config.json:   0%|          | 0.00/239 [00:00<?, ?B/s]"],"application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json":{"version_major":2,"version_minor":0,"model_id":"ffd1ffe1edb045b59a22123e6c956f47"}},"metadata":{}}],"source":["# Choosing the model\n","model_id = \"Qwen/Qwen3-1.7B\"\n","\n","# Load the model faster with bitsandbytes\n","compute_dtype = getattr(torch, \"float16\")\n","\n","bnb_config = BitsAndBytesConfig(\n","    load_in_4bit=True,\n","    bnb_4bit_use_double_quant=False,\n","    bnb_4bit_quant_type=\"nf4\",\n","    bnb_4bit_compute_dtype=compute_dtype,\n",")\n","\n","# Loading the generative model\n","model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n","    model_id,\n","    device_map=device,\n","    torch_dtype=compute_dtype,\n","    quantization_config=bnb_config,\n","    # attn_implementation='eager'   # uncomment for Gemma models\n",")\n","\n","DEVICE, _, _ = get_backend() # automatically detects the underlying device type (CUDA, CPU, XPU, MPS, etc.)"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"uda86S1Pjx2C"},"outputs":[],"source":["# Load the model's tokenizer\n","tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(model_id, padding_side=\"left\")\n","tokenizer.pad_token = tokenizer.eos_token  # Most LLMs don't have a pad token by default\n","\n","# Create our prompts for the model to fill\n","# Let's make some test sentences for the model to fill in:\n","prompts = [\"Our story starts in\", \"The protagonist is\"]\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["##**Method 1:** use model.generate()\n","\n","Documentation: https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/main/en/main_classes/text_generation"],"metadata":{"id":"88XYgWfAzuxR"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# We tokenize our inputs\n","model_inputs = tokenizer(prompts, return_tensors=\"pt\", padding=True).to(DEVICE)\n","\n","# We ask the model to generate answers\n","generated_ids = model.generate(**model_inputs,\n","                                max_new_tokens = 20, # how many new tokens to predict\n","                                )\n","\n","\n","# We decode the model's output\n","results = tokenizer.batch_decode(generated_ids, skip_special_tokens=True)\n","\n","for result in results:\n","  print(result)"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"p2sMZ-BSxBPZ","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1759699786165,"user_tz":-120,"elapsed":2310,"user":{"displayName":"Lena Metheniti","userId":"14049627351271508006"}},"outputId":"e1272139-2ed9-437f-b70a-498da63d0f79"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Our story starts in the 1970s, when the world was still a little more forgiving. The idea\n","The protagonist is a 12-year-old boy who is the only one in his family, and he is very\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["## Method 2: Using the Huggingface pipeline for text generation\n","\n","Documentation: https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/en/main_classes/pipelines"],"metadata":{"id":"v5gMBHTG0KjS"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# Calling the pipeline\n","\n","generation_pipeline = pipeline(task = \"text-generation\",\n","                        model = model,\n","                        tokenizer = tokenizer,\n","                        max_new_tokens = 20, # how many new tokens to predict\n","                       )\n","\n","pipe_results = generation_pipeline(prompts)\n","\n","# The output of the pipeline is structured a bit differently\n","# Each sentence is in a list and the list has a dictionary\n","# Our generated text is in a dictionary under the key \"generated_text\"\n","\n","for output in pipe_results:\n","  print(output[0]['generated_text'])\n"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"FU3BjmlYvSDH","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1759699798043,"user_tz":-120,"elapsed":3257,"user":{"displayName":"Lena Metheniti","userId":"14049627351271508006"}},"outputId":"82a5de82-3ce3-4da2-8f02-3dc473998416"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stderr","text":["Device set to use cuda\n"]},{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Our story starts in the village of Bajada, in the Andes, where the people have lived for generations in\n","The protagonist is a young man named Riven, who is a former soldier. He is a self-proclaimed \"\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"9Is0hOJXlSm7"},"source":["## Generate a story with any method\n","\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["As we saw, the generated text includes half-finished sentences. We can process our outputs so that we have a concise text!"],"metadata":{"id":"J5a5r0RLKgDU"}},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"8udbK9yRlO0u"},"outputs":[],"source":["## Homework!\n","\n","## A function to only keep the first sentence in the generated text\n","\n","def clean_output(generated_sentence):\n","\n","  first_sentence = generated_sentence.split('.')[0] + '.'\n","  return first_sentence"]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# An example with the \"pipeline\" method\n","\n","for output in pipe_results:\n","  print(clean_output(output[0]['generated_text']))"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"Xi-wvGBALXMT","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1759699808731,"user_tz":-120,"elapsed":16,"user":{"displayName":"Lena Metheniti","userId":"14049627351271508006"}},"outputId":"bb9f65b0-9215-4aba-9cbc-e4dc7eec565d"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Our story starts in the village of Bajada, in the Andes, where the people have lived for generations in.\n","The protagonist is a young man named Riven, who is a former soldier.\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["There are two problems with this!\n","\n","First, as we said in the NLP lecture, a sentence doesn't always end in '.' ! Can you improve the function to not make that mistake, based on what we saw in the NLP lecture?\n","\n","Second, the model might have not predicted enough tokens to complete the sentence."],"metadata":{"id":"O2cyzlXcLqxy"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"q2xQLV23lglh"},"source":["## Change the generation strategies to make it better"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### **Greedy Search**\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"pFJ6VFycJNWF"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Greedy search simply selects the word with the highest probability as its next word: $w_t = argmax_{w}P(w | w_{1:t-1})$ at each timestep $t$. The following sketch shows greedy search.\n","\n","![Greedy Search](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/patrickvonplaten/scientific_images/master/greedy_search.png)\n","\n","By default, HF models do **greedy search**. What we have done above included it! So let's use other methods now."],"metadata":{"id":"yknqYf4ER0l5"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### **Beam search**\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"WHsp_DEGJjtV"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Beam search reduces the risk of missing hidden high probability word sequences by keeping the most likely `num_beams` of hypotheses at each time step and eventually choosing the hypothesis that has the overall highest probability. Let's illustrate with `num_beams=2`:\n","\n","![Beam search](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/patrickvonplaten/scientific_images/master/beam_search.png)\n","\n","At time step $1$, besides the most likely hypothesis $\\text{\"The\", \"woman\"}$, beam search also keeps track of the second most likely one $\\text{\"The\", \"dog\"}$. At time step $2$, beam search finds that the word sequence $\\text{\"The\", \"dog\", \"has\"}$ has with $0.36$ a higher probability than $\\text{\"The\", \"nice\", \"woman\"}$, which has $0.2$. Great, it has found the most likely word sequence in our toy example!\n","\n","Beam search will always find an output sequence with higher probability than greedy search, but is not guaranteed to find the most likely output.\n","\n","Let's see how beam search can be used in `transformers`. We set `num_beams > 1` and `early_stopping=True` so that generation is finished when all beam hypotheses reached the EOS token."],"metadata":{"id":"HrcMrTO9RxTm"}},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"wqrihZFdlxkr","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1759699656019,"user_tz":-120,"elapsed":4626,"user":{"displayName":"Lena Metheniti","userId":"14049627351271508006"}},"outputId":"ccd89dcf-56db-4280-f1c0-d1430da03e16"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Our story starts in the early 1900s, in a small town on the edge of the Canadian prairies. This is a place where the land is flat, the sky is vast, and the people are proud of their traditions. The town is\n","The protagonist is a young woman who has been through a lot in her life. She has a strong sense of justice and is driven by a desire to make the world a better place. Her journey is one of self-discovery and personal growth, as she learns to\n"]}],"source":["# We are using method 1, but these arguments can be used for method 2 as well\n","\n","beam_output = model.generate(**model_inputs, # same as the first method\n","                              num_beams=5,\n","                              no_repeat_ngram_size=2, # set no_repeat_ngram_size to 2\n","                              early_stopping=True,\n","                              max_new_tokens = 50, # how many new tokens to predict\n","                              top_p = 0.7, # how many possible words to consider (0-1)\n","                              temperature = 0.9 # Control the probability distribution (0-1)\n","                          )\n","\n","beam_results = tokenizer.batch_decode(beam_output, skip_special_tokens=True)\n","\n","for result in beam_results:\n","  print(result)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["You will notice that we keep getting very similar results, despite the other parameters...\n","\n","In open-ended generation, a couple of reasons have recently been brought forward why beam search might not be the best possible option:\n","\n","- Beam search can work very well in tasks where the length of the desired generation is more or less predictable as in machine translation or summarization - see [Murray et al. (2018)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.10006) and [Yang et al. (2018)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.09582). But this is not the case for open-ended generation where the desired output length can vary greatly, e.g. dialog and story generation.\n","\n","- We have seen that beam search heavily suffers from repetitive generation. This is especially hard to control with *n-gram*- or other penalties in story generation since finding a good trade-off between forced \"no-repetition\" and repeating cycles of identical *n-grams* requires a lot of finetuning.\n","\n","- As argued in [Ari Holtzman et al. (2019)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.09751), high quality human language does not follow a distribution of high probability next words. In other words, as humans, we want generated text to surprise us and not to be boring/predictable. The authors show this nicely by plotting the probability, a model would give to human text vs. what beam search does."],"metadata":{"id":"QBSu10y5ODu8"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### Sampling"],"metadata":{"id":"HBpoH120Okfv"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["In its most basic form, sampling means randomly picking the next word $w_t$ according to its conditional probability distribution:\n","\n","$$w_t \\sim P(w|w_{1:t-1})$$\n","\n","Taking the example from above, the following graphic visualizes language generation when sampling.\n","\n","![vanilla_sampling](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/patrickvonplaten/scientific_images/master/sampling_search.png)\n","\n","It becomes obvious that language generation using sampling is not *deterministic* anymore. The word\n","$\\text{\"car\"}$ is sampled from the conditioned probability distribution $P(w | \\text{\"The\"})$, followed by sampling $\\text{\"drives\"}$ from $P(w | \\text{\"The\"}, \\text{\"car\"})$.\n","\n","In `transformers`, we set `do_sample=True` and deactivate *Top-K* sampling (more on this later) via `top_k=0`. In the following, we will fix `random_seed=0` for illustration purposes. Feel free to change the `random_seed` to play around with the model.\n"],"metadata":{"id":"fF7PAcBNOmGp"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# set seed to reproduce results. Feel free to change the seed though to get different results\n","torch.manual_seed(0)\n","\n","# activate sampling and deactivate top_k by setting top_k sampling to 0\n","sample_output = model.generate(\n","                              **model_inputs,\n","                              do_sample=True,\n","                              max_new_tokens = 20, # how many new tokens to predict\n","                              top_p = 0.7, # how many possible words to consider (0-1)\n","                              temperature = 0.9 # Control the probability distribution (0-1)\n","                              )\n","\n","\n","results = tokenizer.batch_decode(sample_output, skip_special_tokens=True)\n","\n","for result in results:\n","  print(result)"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"fc_w0CCzOwrR","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1759699084910,"user_tz":-120,"elapsed":1809,"user":{"displayName":"Lena Metheniti","userId":"14049627351271508006"}},"outputId":"1ba62c90-038f-4088-e6b1-0dcf486480b8"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Our story starts in a small town in the mountains of the United States. This town is known for its unique, colorful\n","The protagonist is a 15-year-old girl named Lila who is a gifted but isolated prodigy with a\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### Temperature\n","\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"BSbRu7uGPaKR"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["\n","A trick is to make the distribution $P(w|w_{1:t-1})$ sharper (increasing the likelihood of high probability words and decreasing the likelihood of low probability words) by lowering the so-called `temperature` of the [softmax](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Softmax_function#Smooth_arg_max).\n","\n","An illustration of applying temperature to our example from above could look as follows.\n","\n","![top_p_sampling](https://github.com/patrickvonplaten/scientific_images/blob/master/sampling_search_with_temp.png?raw=true)\n","\n","The conditional next word distribution of step $t=1$ becomes much sharper leaving almost no chance for word $\\text{\"car\"}$ to be selected."],"metadata":{"id":"RCOitXYZRhsx"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["temp_output = model.generate(\n","                              **model_inputs,\n","                              max_new_tokens = 20, # how many new tokens to predict\n","                              top_p = 0.7, # how many possible words to consider (0-1)\n","                              temperature = 0.1 # Control the probability distribution (0-1)\n","                              )\n","\n","\n","results = tokenizer.batch_decode(temp_output, skip_special_tokens=True)\n","\n","for result in results:\n","  print(result)"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"h75mVtDqP3YW","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1759699341534,"user_tz":-120,"elapsed":1696,"user":{"displayName":"Lena Metheniti","userId":"14049627351271508006"}},"outputId":"eedb6038-7207-4cd4-be24-7b2b57d9c4ac"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Our story starts in the 1950s, when the first of the five major U.S. airlines,\n","The protagonist is a young woman named Lina, who is a former soldier who has been in a coma for a\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### **Top-K Sampling**\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"-OMH4K-OOsaA"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["[Fan et. al (2018)](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.04833.pdf) introduced a simple, but very powerful sampling scheme, called ***Top-K*** sampling. In *Top-K* sampling, the *K* most likely next words are filtered and the probability mass is redistributed among only those *K* next words.\n","GPT2 adopted this sampling scheme, which was one of the reasons for its success in story generation.\n","\n","We extend the range of words used for both sampling steps in the example above from 3 words to 10 words to better illustrate *Top-K* sampling.\n","\n","![top_k_sampling](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/patrickvonplaten/scientific_images/master/top_k_sampling.png)\n","\n","Having set $K = 6$, in both sampling steps we limit our sampling pool to 6 words. While the 6 most likely words, defined as $V_{\\text{top-K}}$ encompass only *ca.* two-thirds of the whole probability mass in the first step, it includes almost all of the probability mass in the second step. Nevertheless, we see that it successfully eliminates the rather weird candidates $\\text{\"not\", \"the\", \"small\", \"told\"}$\n","in the second sampling step.\n","\n","\n","Let's see how *Top-K* can be used in the library by setting `top_k=50`:"],"metadata":{"id":"qJG6QOwcRo0a"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["topk_output = model.generate(\n","                              **model_inputs,\n","                              max_new_tokens = 20, # how many new tokens to predict\n","                              top_k = 50\n","                              )\n","\n","\n","results = tokenizer.batch_decode(topk_output, skip_special_tokens=True)\n","\n","for result in results:\n","  print(result)"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"kCQLNh6eQS6Z","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1759699379019,"user_tz":-120,"elapsed":2103,"user":{"displayName":"Lena Metheniti","userId":"14049627351271508006"}},"outputId":"0a3b969e-fa45-42d6-9603-7af14ad7eb26"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Our story starts in the spring of 1984, when the American university was in the throes of the\n","The protagonist is a 13-year-old boy named Max who lives with his family in a small town, and\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### **Top-p (nucleus) sampling**\n"],"metadata":{"id":"HkI5BSrZQStZ"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["\n","Instead of sampling only from the most likely *K* words, in *Top-p* sampling chooses from the smallest possible set of words whose cumulative probability exceeds the probability *p*. The probability mass is then redistributed among this set of words. This way, the size of the set of words (*a.k.a* the number of words in the set) can dynamically increase and decrease according to the next word's probability distribution. Ok, that was very wordy, let's visualize.\n","\n","![top_p_sampling](https://github.com/patrickvonplaten/scientific_images/blob/master/top_p_sampling.png?raw=true)\n","\n","Having set $p=0.92$, *Top-p* sampling picks the *minimum* number of words to exceed together $p=92\\%$ of the probability mass, defined as $V_{\\text{top-p}}$. In the first example, this included the 9 most likely words, whereas it only has to pick the top 3 words in the second example to exceed 92%. Quite simple actually! It can be seen that it keeps a wide range of words where the next word is arguably less predictable, *e.g.* $P(w | \\text{\"The\"})$, and only a few words when the next word seems more predictable, *e.g.* $P(w | \\text{\"The\", \"car\"})$.\n","\n","Alright, time to check it out in `transformers`!\n","We activate *Top-p* sampling by setting `0 < top_p < 1`:"],"metadata":{"id":"ctvabtbuRr3o"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["topp_output = model.generate(\n","                              **model_inputs,\n","